\ core::iterator -- the linear iteration protocol (HKT as the associated-type
\ substitute; P7b.S8 R1). `Iterator['It: * -> *]` makes the iterator itself
\ the type constructor rather than naming an associated element type: `next`
\ consumes the iterator and yields the element plus the advanced iterator
\ packed together in one `Step` value. `Done` carries nothing, so the
\ exhausted case's final drop lives inside `next`'s own `Done` arm -- the one
\ canonical site, never a remainder construction (this is why the S6
\ construction wall, `src/check/poly.rs:6193`, is never reached by any
\ shape in this module). The Option-shaped alternative
\ (`next ( 'It['T] -- Option['T] 'It['T] )`) was considered and rejected: arm
\ parity would force the dead iterator out to every caller and the final drop
\ to every `None` path instead of living in one place.
import: intrinsics | drop dup swap len sub >usize | ;
import: self::prelude | if eq | ;
import: self::list | List Nil Cons | ;

\ `next` is deliberately absent from this list: a trait member's own
\ synthesized word is named `member;Trait;module;Type` (never the bare
\ member name, `src/parser.rs`'s `synth_member_word_name`), so `export:`
\ has no bare `next` to promise -- `exportable_names` only ever sees the
\ mangled spelling. A consumer imports the trait and its ctors
\ (`import: core::iterator | Step Done More Iterator | ;`) and does *not*
\ name `next`; bare `next` then resolves through trait dispatch at the
\ call site.
export: Step Done More Iterator ;
\ `for_each` and `fold` are ordinary exported words, imported by name. A
\ consumer that only *calls* them needs nothing else -- the
\ `Step Done More Iterator` surface above is required only of a consumer
\ that writes its own `Step?` dispatch arms or its own `Iterator`-bounded
\ word (measured, P7b.S8 phase 2).
export: for_each fold ;

type: Step['T 'Rest]
| Done
| More 'T 'Rest
;

trait: Iterator['It: * -> *]
  : next ( 'It['T] -- Step['T 'It['T]] ) ;
;

\ The `Cons` arm destructures the matched list (`Cons>` disposes of the
\ matched cell and hands back its fields, element deepest, the still-cell-
\ wrapped remainder on top) and packs `More` with the element deepest and
\ the (unwrapped) remainder on top -- the same field order `Cons` itself
\ constructs with. The exhausted `Nil` arm consumes the matched (empty) list
\ via `drop` and returns only the `Done` shell: no remainder to construct, so
\ the S6 wall is never in play here.
impl: Iterator for List
  : next
    ~[ ( Nil ) drop Done ]
    ~[ ( Cons ) Cons> | v rest | v rest ^> More ]
    List? ;
;

\ The built-in-view impl (P7b.S6d REQ-6). It lives here rather than beside a
\ `Slice` declaration because `Slice` is a built-in type declaring no module,
\ so the impl-placement gate's co-declaration arm is structurally
\ unavailable and `Iterator`'s own module is the only legal home (the gate
\ itself stays: a foreign-module impl of the imported trait still refuses,
\ P7b.S6d G19). The target is the shared `Slice[i64]` only -- the mutable
\ `!Slice[i64]` impl is closed by Ruling A's enum-payload sweep, which fires
\ first at the `Step` declaration (SOO-45). The body is the natural one over
\ `len`/`&>`/`subslice`: read the element through a shared borrow, advance
\ with an O(1) sub-view. The member spells `inline` per-impl (REQ-3): the
\ member-output ban forces the inline spelling (a `Step` carrying a view
\ may not cross a non-inline member's return boundary), and trait-level
\ inline is not an option -- it would re-route the List/Range members through
\ the poly-combinator path, where the nullary ctors mis-resolve (S6d-8.5),
\ so the keyword lives on this impl's member alone. The straight-line Done
\ arm (`~[ drop Done ]`, no helper) is the natural body: the More arm's Step
\ carries the remainder view's deriv -- rootless, the receiver being a
\ parameter, so the naming mint has no owned root -- while the Done arm's
\ nullary Step carries none, and the borrow join unions that rootless
\ one-sided asymmetry (P7b.S6d REQ-5) instead of refusing. Co-locating the
\ impl mints the `Step[i64 Slice[i64]]` monomorph for every importer -- the
\ second monomorph of the family List consumers already see -- and the
\ bare-name candidate lookup unions the live check-time mints so each site
\ resolves to its own (REQ-4; the G7/G8 goldens pin it).
impl: Iterator for Slice[i64]
  : next inline
    | s |
    s dup len |n|
    n 0 >usize eq
    ~[ drop Done ]
    ~[
      dup 0 >usize &> @ |v|
      1 >usize n 1 >usize sub subslice
      v swap More
    ]
    if ;
;

\ The consumers (P7b.S8 R4). Written once against the `Iterator` bound: they
\ name no List word, so every future impl gets them for free. The self-call
\ sits in tail position in the `More` arm, so the P7.S3g transform turns the
\ recursion into a loop back-edge and the drain runs in one frame
\ (`src/ir/driver.rs:1093`). The `Done` arm drops only the `Step` shell: the
\ exhausted iterator was already disposed of inside `next`'s own `Done` arm,
\ the protocol's one canonical final-drop site. Leaving that shell undropped
\ is a compile error (the variant-escape rule), which is what makes the
\ protocol's linearity enforced rather than merely intended.
: for_each ['It: Iterator 'T] ( 'It['T] [ 'T -- ] -- )
  | f |
  next
  ~[ ( Done ) drop ]
  ~[ ( More ) More> | v rest | v f call rest f for_each ]
  Step? ;

\ Accumulator under the element, matching `lib/core/combinators.sth`'s array
\ `fold`, so a call reads `acc [ ... ] fold`.
: fold ['It: Iterator 'T 'A] ( 'It['T] 'A [ 'A 'T -- 'A ] -- 'A )
  | f | | acc |
  next
  ~[ ( Done ) drop acc ]
  ~[ ( More ) More> | v rest | rest acc v f call f fold ]
  Step? ;
